virtio-fs (macOS): keep the guest inside the shared directory

The macOS passthrough builds host paths as /.vol/<dev>/<ino>/<name> from the
names the guest sends, without filtering them, and open_inode drops
O_NOFOLLOW. A guest that controls its kernel (root) could look up "..", send a
name with "/" in it, or open a symlink it planted, and so reach any host path
the launcher's user can. Reject ".", ".." and "/" in every name that becomes a
host path (name_to_path, and do_unlink's unlinkat), and keep O_NOFOLLOW when
opening an inode.

Applied by nixos-gpu-vm's build.sh after steamac's libkrun patches.

--- a/src/devices/src/virtio/fs/macos/passthrough.rs
+++ b/src/devices/src/virtio/fs/macos/passthrough.rs
@@ -159,6 +159,16 @@
 
 fn ebadf() -> io::Error {
     linux_error(io::Error::from_raw_os_error(libc::EBADF))
+}
+
+/// A name from the guest must be a single component of the shared directory: `.`, `..` or a
+/// `/` would let it address paths outside the share, since the host resolves them as given.
+fn check_name(name: &CStr) -> io::Result<()> {
+    let bytes = name.to_bytes();
+    if bytes.is_empty() || bytes == b"." || bytes == b".." || bytes.contains(&b'/') {
+        return Err(einval());
+    }
+    Ok(())
 }
 
 fn einval() -> io::Error {
@@ -717,6 +727,7 @@
     }
 
     fn name_to_path(&self, parent: Inode, name: &CStr) -> io::Result<CString> {
+        check_name(name)?;
